<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Biram</h1><p class="st-meta">1000 CE – 1805 CE (approx.) · capital Biram</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Hausa state east of Kano, traditionally the oldest of the seven Hausa kingdoms and seat of their legendary founder's eldest son.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Biram, centred on Garun Gabas, appears in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayajidda">Bayajidda</a> legend: the hero is said to have had a son named Biram by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornu">Bornu</a> princess before reaching <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-daura">Daura</a>, so the kingdom was counted first among the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/hausa-kingdoms">Hausa Bakwai</a>. Its origins probably lie in the 11th century, on the borderland between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> plains and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kanem-bornu-empire">Kanem-Bornu Empire</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Tradition gives Biram the role of seat of government among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa states</a>, but in practice it was small and strongly influenced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornu">Bornu</a>, to which it paid tribute for long periods. Its records are sparse, and its rulers are known only from late lists.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> forces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> overran Biram in the first decade of the 19th century, and its lands were incorporated into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hadejia-emirate">Hadejia Emirate</a>. Garun Gabas survives as a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jigawa-state">Jigawa State</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-biram.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Biram" width="320" height="217"><figcaption>Image: Sani Ahmad Usman · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Another_site_of_River_Hadejia_Jigawa_state_Nigeria.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1000 CE – 1805 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Biram</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Islam and Hausa traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hausa</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/nigeria">Nigeria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Biram, Garun Gabas</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1402&amp;focus=kingdom-of-biram" aria-label="Kingdom of Biram on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4956 1714 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Biram lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5276 1970L5144 1947M5276 1970L5399 1946M5276 1970L5276 1970"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5144 1947h0M5399 1946h0M5276 1970h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5276 1970h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5276 1970h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1402&amp;focus=kingdom-of-biram">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
